<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">ScoreGate: Adaptive Chunk Selection for
<br class="ltx_break">Retrieval-Augmented Generation
<br class="ltx_break">via Dual-Score Statistical Fusion</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">
<span id="id1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_top">
<span class="ltx_tbody">
<span id="id1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="id1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">Karamvir Singh</span>
<span id="id1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left">Arvind Jain</span></span>
<span id="id1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="id1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><span id="id1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="font-size:90%;">Staff AI Engineer</span></span>
<span id="id1.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left"><span id="id1.2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="font-size:90%;">Senior Engineering Manager</span></span></span>
<span id="id1.3" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="id1.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><span id="id1.3.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">HighLevel, Inc.</span></span>
<span id="id1.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left"><span id="id1.3.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">HighLevel, Inc.</span></span></span>
</span>
</span>
</span><span id="id2" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_correspondence ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span>Correspondence: <span id="id2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">karamvir.singh@gohighlevel.com</span></span></span></span><span id="id3" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_note ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span><span id="id3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">arvind.jain@gohighlevel.com</span></span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Fixed-cardinality retrieval injects a constant top-<math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> chunks into the
generator regardless of query complexity, causing over-retrieval for
narrow queries and under-retrieval for compositional ones.
We describe <span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">ScoreGate</span>, a lightweight score-space decision
mechanism that controls <em id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">retrieval cardinality</em> at inference time
using two scores already produced by the standard pipeline: bi-encoder
similarity <math id="abstract1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and cross-encoder reranker score <math id="abstract1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, with no
additional model inference calls required.
Its core insight is that cross-encoder affirmation can rescue
semantically relevant chunks that bi-encoder retrieval ranks poorly
due to vocabulary mismatch—a failure mode unaddressed by fixed-<math id="abstract1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>
or single-score thresholding.
On MS MARCO (200 dev queries), ScoreGate achieves MRR@10 = 0.401
with 35% fewer retained chunks than Standard Top-K.
On an internal benchmark (<math id="abstract1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\!=\!300" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.108em">=</mo><mn>300</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\!=\!300</annotation></semantics></math>, Fleiss’ <math id="abstract1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\kappa}\!=\!0.87" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>κ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.108em">=</mo><mn>0.87</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\kappa}\!=\!0.87</annotation></semantics></math>),
ScoreGate observed zero false positives (95% CI [96.4%, 100%]) at
97.77–99.34% recall, with 34.8% fewer tokens per query and only
31 ms added latency.
Results on both MS MARCO and real-world production traffic suggest
that adaptive retrieval cardinality can improve retrieval efficiency
without degrading retrieval quality.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Keywords:<span id="p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> ScoreGate Retrieval-Augmented Generation Adaptive Chunk Selection Dual-Score Fusion Cross-Encoder Reranking Production RAG</span></span></p>
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_section" style="font-size:120%;">1  Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) augments a language model prompt with
passages retrieved from an external corpus, enabling document-grounded
responses <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>.
The retrieval stage operates in two phases: a <em id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">bi-encoder</em> embeds the
query and corpus documents into a shared vector space and retrieves the
top-<math id="S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> candidates by cosine similarity; a <em id="S1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">cross-encoder reranker</em>
scores each candidate by performing full query–document attention, producing
measurably more accurate relevance estimates than embedding similarity
alone <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>.
The generator then receives a fixed-cardinality subset of top-<math id="S1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> chunks,
where <math id="S1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> is a hyperparameter set before deployment.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The fixed-<math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> constraint imposes a hard upper bound on retrieval cardinality
that is mismatched to query complexity.
A navigational query such as “<em id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">What is the refund policy?</em>”
is fully answered by a single chunk, whereas a compositional query such as
“<em id="S1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Compare plans A and B across pricing, features, and cancellation
terms</em>” may require eight or more chunks.
Setting <math id="S1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> conservatively causes coverage gaps on broad queries;
setting it liberally injects low-relevance distractors that increase
generation error rate and token cost <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>.
Neither a larger nor a smaller <math id="S1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> resolves this tension because the optimal
cardinality is a function of the query, not a constant.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Existing approaches address retrieval <em id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">quality</em> but
generally do not treat cardinality as a first-class decision.
Rerankers reorder candidates but do not select a subset.
LLM-based chunk filters <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite> prompt a language model for a binary
keep/discard decision per chunk, achieving good precision but introducing an
additional inference call per query.
Single-score threshold cutoffs are unreliable: high embedding similarity does
not guarantee contextual relevance, and low embedding similarity does not
exclude relevance (paraphrastic references use different vocabulary).</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We observe that the <em id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">joint</em> score pair <math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(s_{i},r_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(s_{i},r_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> carries
measurably richer signal than either score in isolation.
The two scores are complementary: <math id="S1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> captures lexical and semantic
proximity in the embedding space, while <math id="S1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> captures contextual fit via
full cross-attention.
Chunks where both scores agree can be classified deterministically;
chunks where they disagree require an empirically motivated fusion strategy.
We observe a further structural failure in standard pipelines:
bi-encoder retrieval systematically under-ranks chunks that are
semantically relevant but expressed using vocabulary absent from
the query (paraphrases, domain-specific synonyms, indirect
references).
The cross-encoder reranker, which performs full query–document
attention, often correctly scores these chunks as relevant despite
their low embedding similarity.
A single-score threshold on either <math id="S1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> cannot recover
these chunks reliably.
The joint score pair <math id="S1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(s_{i},r_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(s_{i},r_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> carries the signal needed to
identify and rescue them.
This motivates <span id="S1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">ScoreGate</span>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Design and contributions.</h4>

<div id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">ScoreGate is a lightweight score-space decision mechanism rather
than a new retrieval architecture.
It slots into any existing two-stage RAG pipeline without
architectural changes, additional training, or extra model
inference calls.
Its main design contributions are:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Dual-score bucket classification.</span>
A four-region partition of the <math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(s_{i},r_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(s_{i},r_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> score space, with
deterministic rules for agreement regions and threshold-based fusion
for disagreement regions—requiring no model calls beyond the
standard reranker.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Asymmetric fusion thresholds.</span>
Bucket-specific retention thresholds
(<math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{B2}\!=\!0.255" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.108em">=</mo><mn>0.255</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{B2}\!=\!0.255</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{B3}\!=\!0.15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.108em">=</mo><mn>0.15</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{B3}\!=\!0.15</annotation></semantics></math>) derived from the
observation that reranker-affirmed chunks in B3 are more likely true
positives than reranker-rejected chunks in B2 at equivalent fusion
scores.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Empirical validation on real-world data.</span>
Observed precision within 95% CI [96.4%, 100%] at 97.77–99.34% recall (vs. 91.11–92.41% for LLM
filtering), 34.8% token reduction, and only 31 ms added latency on
a 300-triple annotated benchmark and a real-world query dataset of
1,247 live queries.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Production safety.</span>
A MAX-<math id="S1.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> ceiling prevents context-window overflow and preserves
backward compatibility with fixed-context downstream components.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_section" style="font-size:120%;">2  Related Work</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsection">2.1  Retrieval-Augmented Generation</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Lewis et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite> established RAG as a paradigm for knowledge-intensive NLP.
Subsequent work extended RAG to iterative retrieval–generation
loops <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite> and pre-/post-retrieval optimisation
strategies <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>.
Wang et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite> showed that injecting low-relevance passages degrades
generation quality proportionally to the fraction of irrelevant content in
the context window; they address this via learned context filtering at generation
time.
A concurrent line of work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite> addresses the same problem via
query-oriented passage scoring before the generator sees any context.
ScoreGate is complementary to both: we determine <em id="S2.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">how many</em> chunks
to pass to the generator, rather than filtering or reranking within a
fixed-cardinality set.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsection">2.2  Bi-Encoder Retrieval and Cross-Encoder Reranking</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Bi-encoders retrieve by nearest-neighbor search over pre-computed document
vectors <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>.
Cross-encoder rerankers compute a relevance score via full self-attention
over the query–document pair, producing more accurate estimates at higher
cost <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>.
ColBERT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite> achieves a favourable accuracy–efficiency
trade-off using token-level late interaction.
In production RAG, the standard architecture is bi-encoder retrieval of
top-<math id="S2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> candidates followed by cross-encoder reranking to produce
top-<math id="S2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> chunks for the generator.
ScoreGate intervenes at this second stage, replacing fixed-<math id="S2.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> truncation
with score-adaptive selection.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsection">2.3  Adaptive and Conditional Retrieval</h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">FLARE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite> conditions retrieval triggering on generator
token-level confidence.
Self-RAG <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite> trains the generator to emit control tokens
indicating retrieval necessity and passage relevance.
Both methods address retrieval <em id="S2.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">triggering</em> (when to retrieve);
ScoreGate addresses retrieval <em id="S2.SS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">cardinality</em> (how many to
retain)—orthogonal problems that can be composed.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsection">2.4  Adaptive Retrieval Cardinality</h3>

<div id="S2.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A small body of work addresses dynamic stopping and cardinality
control in retrieval.
<em id="S2.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Score-gap stopping</em> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite> truncates the ranked
list when the score gap <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{i}-r_{i+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{i}-r_{i+1}</annotation></semantics></math> exceeds a threshold,
exploiting the intuition that large gaps signal relevance
boundaries.
<em id="S2.SS4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Confidence-based retrieval</em> in Self-RAG <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>
conditions the number of retrieved passages on token-level
generation confidence.
<em id="S2.SS4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Adaptive truncation</em> methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite> determine
retrieval necessity from entity popularity, bypassing cardinality
decisions for well-memorised facts.
ScoreGate is complementary to these approaches: it operates
after reranking using scores already computed, requires no
additional model calls, and specifically targets the
vocabulary-mismatch recovery problem unaddressed by gap-based
or confidence-based methods.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S2.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 2.4 Adaptive Retrieval Cardinality ‣ 2 Related Work ‣ ScoreGate: Adaptive Chunk Selection forRetrieval-Augmented Generationvia Dual-Score Statistical Fusion" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> positions ScoreGate relative to
common retrieval methods.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>
<span id="S2.T1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Method comparison.</span>
