</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsection">6.6  Hallucination Reduction</h3>

<div id="S6.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We additionally conducted a hallucination analysis on 300 generated
answers.
A hallucination was defined as a factual claim unsupported by either
the retrieved context or the underlying source document.
Three annotators independently labelled hallucination presence
(inter-annotator agreement: Fleiss’ <math id="S6.SS6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\kappa}\!=\!0.79" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>κ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.108em">=</mo><mn>0.79</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\kappa}\!=\!0.79</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.T15" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 15: </span>
<span id="S6.T15.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Hallucination rate comparison</span> (<math id="S6.T15.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\!=\!300" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.108em">=</mo><mn>300</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\!=\!300</annotation></semantics></math> generated
answers; Fleiss’ <math id="S6.T15.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\kappa}\!=\!0.79" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>κ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.108em">=</mo><mn>0.79</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\kappa}\!=\!0.79</annotation></semantics></math>).
ScoreGate reduces hallucination incidence by 4.7 percentage
points relative to Standard Top-K by preventing low-relevance
context from reaching the generator.
</figcaption>
<table id="S6.T15.4" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S6.T15.4.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S6.T15.4.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt"><span id="S6.T15.4.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Method</span></th>
<th id="S6.T15.4.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S6.T15.4.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Hallucination Rate</span></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S6.T15.4.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S6.T15.4.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t">Standard Top-K</th>
<td id="S6.T15.4.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">11.8%</td></tr>
<tr id="S6.T15.4.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S6.T15.4.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb">ScoreGate (ours)</th>
<td id="S6.T15.4.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S6.T15.4.3.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">7.1%</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="S6.SS6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">ScoreGate reduced hallucination incidence from 11.8% to 7.1%
relative to Standard Top-K, suggesting that adaptive retrieval
cardinality may reduce distractor-induced generation errors.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS7" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsection">6.7  Threats to Validity</h3>

<div id="S6.SS7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Internal validity.</span>
Thresholds <math id="S6.SS7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.SS7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{r}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.SS7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{B2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{B2}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.SS7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{B3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{B3}</annotation></semantics></math>, and
<math id="S6.SS7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> were derived and evaluated on data from the same production
system.
Although query windows are disjoint and the ARB knowledge base is
acknowledged as shared, temporal correlation in production logs may
reduce true independence.
The 200-query MS MARCO calibration set and 200-query ARB evaluation
set are fully independent.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS7.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS7.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS7.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">External validity.</span>
ScoreGate is validated on one proprietary domain and MS MARCO.
Threshold values (<math id="S6.SS7.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{s}\!=\!0.70" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.108em">=</mo><mn>0.70</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{s}\!=\!0.70</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.SS7.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{r}\!=\!0.08" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.108em">=</mo><mn>0.08</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{r}\!=\!0.08</annotation></semantics></math>) reflect
the score distributions of our specific bi-encoder and cross-encoder
models; different model pairs will produce different distributions
requiring re-calibration.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS7.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS7.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS7.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Construct validity.</span>
Hallucination is measured by annotator agreement on factual claim
support, which may not capture all generation failure modes.
The Avg. Relevance Likert proxy (<math id="S6.SS7.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\!=\!50" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.108em">=</mo><mn>50</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\!=\!50</annotation></semantics></math>) is a weak surrogate
for end-to-end answer quality.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS7.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS7.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS7.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Statistical validity.</span>
McNemar tests assume paired binary outcomes from the same queries.
We confirm this holds for all reported comparisons.
The hallucination reduction (11.8% <math id="S6.SS7.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> 7.1%) is
reported as an observed difference on <math id="S6.SS7.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\!=\!300" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.108em">=</mo><mn>300</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\!=\!300</annotation></semantics></math> answers; a formal
significance test on this proportion difference yields
<math id="S6.SS7.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi^{2}\!=\!4.82" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>χ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.108em">=</mo><mn>4.82</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi^{2}\!=\!4.82</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.SS7.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\!=\!0.028" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.108em">=</mo><mn>0.028</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\!=\!0.028</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS8" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsection">6.8  Production Deployment Characteristics</h3>

<div id="S6.SS8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Real-world retrieval distribution.</span>
In the real-world dataset, irrelevant candidates constituted
approximately 71% of retrieved chunks, consistent with the
expected long-tail retrieval distribution observed in production
RAG systems.
ScoreGate’s four-bucket design is specifically suited to this
regime: the B4 discard region handles the dominant irrelevant
mass, while B3 recovers the sparse but high-value
vocabulary-mismatch chunks that standard filtering misses.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS8.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS8.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS8.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Threshold stability.</span>
Sensitivity analysis across 75 parameter configurations showed
stable behaviour, with zero observed false positives and recall
varying by at most 3.2 percentage points.
Figure <a href="#S6.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 6.8 Production Deployment Characteristics ‣ 6 Discussion ‣ ScoreGate: Adaptive Chunk Selection forRetrieval-Augmented Generationvia Dual-Score Statistical Fusion" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> visualises the <math id="S6.SS8.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\tau_{s},\tau_{r})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\tau_{s},\tau_{r})</annotation></semantics></math>
joint <math id="S6.SS8.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> surface and the <math id="S6.SS8.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> sensitivity curve.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.F6" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2606.14269v1/fig_robustness.png" id="S6.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:452/170;" width="452" height="170" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 6: </span>
<span id="S6.F6.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Left:</span> <math id="S6.F6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> (%) over the <math id="S6.F6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\tau_{s},\tau_{r})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\tau_{s},\tau_{r})</annotation></semantics></math> grid;
star marks the selected point (<math id="S6.F6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{s}\!=\!0.70" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.108em">=</mo><mn>0.70</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{s}\!=\!0.70</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S6.F6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{r}\!=\!0.08" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.108em">=</mo><mn>0.08</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{r}\!=\!0.08</annotation></semantics></math>). Surface <math id="S6.F6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\geq}\,96\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn> 96</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\geq}\,96\%</annotation></semantics></math> throughout.
<span id="S6.F6.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Right:</span> <math id="S6.F6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and Recall vs. <math id="S6.F6.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>, both peaking
at <math id="S6.F6.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\!=\!0.3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.108em">=</mo><mn>0.3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\!=\!0.3</annotation></semantics></math> (dotted line).
</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S6.SS8.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS8.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Bootstrap analysis further showed the optimal single-threshold
baseline remained within <math id="S6.SS8.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta^{*}\in[0.16,0.22]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0.16</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.22</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta^{*}\in[0.16,0.22]</annotation></semantics></math>.
These results indicate that ScoreGate is not highly sensitive
to narrow parameter choices, and that the threshold derivation
procedure produces stable operating points across perturbations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS8.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS8.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS8.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Computational efficiency.</span>
The additional computational cost of ScoreGate is limited to
fusion arithmetic and sorting over <math id="S6.SS8.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\!=\!40" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.108em">=</mo><mn>40</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\!=\!40</annotation></semantics></math> candidates,
adding only 31 ms average latency relative to the standard
retrieval-and-reranking pipeline.
This compares favourably against the LLM Filter baseline,
which requires 40 sequential <span id="S6.SS8.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">gpt-4o-mini</span> inference
calls per query at an estimated latency of <math id="S6.SS8.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\approx" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≈</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\approx</annotation></semantics></math>8,400 ms
— a 19<math id="S6.SS8.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> overhead.
ScoreGate achieves higher recall at a fraction of the
inference cost of the LLM Filter.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS8.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS8.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS8.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Cross-domain transferability.</span>
The threshold derivation procedure is domain-agnostic and
transfers without architectural modification.
On MS MARCO, ScoreGate with original production thresholds
achieves MRR@10 = 0.392, outperforming Standard Top-K
(MRR@10 = 0.387), and improves further to MRR@10 = 0.401
after domain-specific threshold re-calibration using the
same derivation procedure described in Section 4.1.
The shift in derived thresholds (<math id="S6.SS8.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>: 0.70 <math id="S6.SS8.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\to" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\to</annotation></semantics></math> 0.63;
<math id="S6.SS8.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{r}</annotation></semantics></math>: 0.08 <math id="S6.SS8.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\to" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\to</annotation></semantics></math> 0.11) reflects domain-specific score
distributions and confirms that the calibration procedure
correctly adapts to new corpora without retraining.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS8.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS8.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS8.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Known limitations.</span>
ScoreGate’s performance degrades when the candidate pool itself
has low recall (i.e., the bi-encoder fails to retrieve the
relevant chunk in top-<math id="S6.SS8.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>).
It inherits reranker errors: a cross-encoder that systematically
misscores a domain will propagate those errors.
Multilingual and code-heavy corpora may exhibit score distributions
that require significant threshold re-calibration.
Multi-hop queries—where evidence must be synthesised across
passages—are not evaluated and represent a known open problem.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS8.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS8.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS8.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Future directions.</span>
Evaluation on dedicated multi-hop reasoning benchmarks such as
HotpotQA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite> and MuSiQue remains future work, as
does extension to biomedical, legal, and scientific retrieval
corpora.
An empirically motivated cost-sensitive derivation of <math id="S6.SS8.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{B2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{B2}</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S6.SS8.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{B3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{B3}</annotation></semantics></math> from asymmetric false-positive/false-negative
cost models could replace the current grid-search procedure
and enable automatic per-domain calibration.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_section" style="font-size:120%;">7  Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We presented <span id="S7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">ScoreGate</span>, a practical adaptive retrieval filter
that controls context cardinality in two-stage RAG systems.
Its key mechanism—using cross-encoder affirmation to recover
semantically relevant chunks that bi-encoder retrieval ranks poorly
(the B3 region)—accounts for the majority of observed recall
improvements over both LLM-based filtering and reranker-only thresholding.
On the internal benchmark, observed precision fell within
95% CI [96.4%, 100%] (zero false positives on <math id="S7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\!=\!100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.108em">=</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\!=\!100</annotation></semantics></math>
per category) at 97.77–99.34% recall, and
MRR@10 = 0.401 on MS MARCO, with 34.8% fewer tokens per query.
On a real-world dataset of 1,247 live queries, ScoreGate correctly
abstains when the knowledge base lacks relevant content and returns
non-empty results when retrieval is warranted, with <math id="S7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\approx\!95\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mn>95</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\approx\!95\%</annotation></semantics></math>
overall correctness.
The 34.8% reduction in per-query token consumption translates directly
to lower operational cost at scale.
These results suggest that query-adaptive retrieval cardinality can be
determined practically using scores already available in a standard
two-stage RAG pipeline, without additional model inference.
ScoreGate is best understood as a pragmatic engineering solution:
it combines scores already available in any two-stage pipeline with
an empirically motivated decision rule to achieve measurable
improvements in retrieval efficiency, with results that are stable
under the evaluated conditions.
Additional out-of-domain evaluation (e.g., BEIR subsets for
biomedical and legal retrieval) would strengthen generalisability
claims beyond the current MS MARCO result, which rests on 200
dev queries.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Data Availability</h2>

<div id="Sx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The ARB triples, RWD query logs, and production knowledge base are proprietary and cannot be released.
To facilitate reproduction on other domains, we provide: (1) the complete ARB annotation protocol (category definitions, labelling guidelines, and inter-annotator resolution procedure); (2) the full LLM Filter prompt and decoding configuration (Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B LLM Filter: Prompt and Decoding Configuration ‣ ScoreGate: Adaptive Chunk Selection forRetrieval-Augmented Generationvia Dual-Score Statistical Fusion" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>); (3) the threshold derivation procedure (Section 4.1 and Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B LLM Filter: Prompt and Decoding Configuration ‣ ScoreGate: Adaptive Chunk Selection forRetrieval-Augmented Generationvia Dual-Score Statistical Fusion" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>); and (4) the ScoreGate algorithm in full (Algorithm 1).
A researcher with access to any two-stage RAG pipeline (bi-encoder + cross-encoder) can replicate the full experimental protocol using domain-specific query logs following the procedures described in Section 5.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Acknowledgements</h2>

<div id="Sx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The authors thank Anshul Chandra (SDE 2), Rohit Singh (SDET 2),
Aniruddha Banerjea (SDE 3), and Pratik Patil (SDE 3) for their
contributions to system implementation, evaluation infrastructure,
and annotation support.
We also thank the HighLevel Engineering Leadership for supporting
this research and providing access to production telemetry data.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Implementation and Reproducibility Details</h2>

<section id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Embedding model.</h4>

<div id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Bi-encoder: <span id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">text-embedding-ada-002</span> (OpenAI), 1536-dim,
cosine similarity, <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-normalised query and chunk vectors.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Reranker model.</h4>

<div id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Cross-encoder: <span id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">cross-encoder/ms-marco-MiniLM-L-6-v2</span>
(Hugging Face), raw logit output normalised per-query via min–max
scaling over the top-<math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> candidate set.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Chunking.</h4>

<div id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Paragraph-boundary splitting, target chunk length 180 tokens
(cl100k_base tokeniser), 20-token overlap between adjacent chunks.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Threshold derivation procedure.</h4>

<div id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">(1) Sample 200 queries from the production log (held-out from ARB).
(2) For each query, retrieve top-<math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\!=\!40" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.108em">=</mo><mn>40</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\!=\!40</annotation></semantics></math> candidates and compute
<math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(s_{i},r_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(s_{i},r_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> pairs.
(3) Set <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> = median <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> across all retrieved pairs.
(4) Set <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> at the 5th percentile of <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> values for
human-annotated relevant chunks on a 50-query labelled subset.
(5) Grid-search <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{B2}\in[0.15,0.35]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0.15</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.35</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{B2}\in[0.15,0.35]</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{B3}\in[0.10,0.25]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0.10</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.25</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{B3}\in[0.10,0.25]</annotation></semantics></math> (step 0.01) maximising <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
(6) Grid-search <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in[0.1,0.7]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0.1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.7</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in[0.1,0.7]</annotation></semantics></math> (step 0.1) maximising
<math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> on the same held-out set.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px5" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Hardware.</h4>

<div id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">AWS <span id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">m5.2xlarge</span> (8 vCPU, 32 GB RAM), single-threaded
inference. Random seed: 42 for all bootstrap resampling.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>LLM Filter: Prompt and Decoding Configuration</h2>

<div id="A2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The LLM Filter baseline uses <span id="A2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">gpt-4o-mini</span> with the following
verbatim prompt (zero-shot, no chain-of-thought):</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<blockquote id="A2.p2.1" class="ltx_quote">
<p id="A2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold">System:
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="A2.p2.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">You are an expert information retrieval and question-answering
<br class="ltx_break">assistant. Your task is to evaluate whether a retrieved passage
<br class="ltx_break">contains information that directly, measurably, or paraphrastically
<br class="ltx_break">helps answer a user query.
<br class="ltx_break" style="--ltx-break-space:4.0pt;"></span></span>
<span id="A2.p2.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Relevance criteria:
<br class="ltx_break">- Mark as Relevant if the passage directly answers the query,
<br class="ltx_break">  provides a key fact needed to answer it, or expresses the
<br class="ltx_break">  answer using different but equivalent terminology.
<br class="ltx_break">- Mark as Not Relevant if the passage only mentions the same
<br class="ltx_break">  topic or entities as the query but does not address the
<br class="ltx_break">  specific question being asked.
<br class="ltx_break">- Mark as Not Relevant if the passage is about a related
<br class="ltx_break">  domain but would not help a reader answer this query.
<br class="ltx_break" style="--ltx-break-space:4.0pt;"></span>
<span id="A2.p2.1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Be strict. Topical overlap alone is not relevance.
<br class="ltx_break">Paraphrastic relevance counts as relevant.
<br class="ltx_break">Do not infer or hallucinate information not in the passage.
<br class="ltx_break" style="--ltx-break-space:6.0pt;"></span>
<span id="A2.p2.1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold">User:
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="A2.p2.1.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">Query: {query}
<br class="ltx_break" style="--ltx-break-space:4.0pt;"></span></span>
<span id="A2.p2.1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Passage:
<br class="ltx_break">"""
<br class="ltx_break">{chunk}
<br class="ltx_break">"""
<br class="ltx_break" style="--ltx-break-space:4.0pt;"></span>
<span id="A2.p2.1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Does this passage contain information that answers or
<br class="ltx_break">measurably helps answer the query?
<br class="ltx_break">Respond with exactly one word: Relevant or Not Relevant.
<br class="ltx_break">Do not explain your reasoning. Do not add punctuation.</span></p>
</blockquote>
</div>
<div id="A2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Decoding configuration:</span></p>
<ul id="A2.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Model: <span id="A2.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">gpt-4o-mini</span> (OpenAI, <span id="A2.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">gpt-4o-mini-2024-07-18</span>)</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Temperature: 0 (greedy decoding)</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Max tokens: 5</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Top-p: 1.0 (not applied at temperature 0)</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Presence penalty: 0, Frequency penalty: 0</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="A2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">A chunk was retained if the model response was <span id="A2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Relevant</span> (case-insensitive match; any response not matching <span id="A2.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Relevant</span> was treated as Not Relevant).
The score pair <math id="A2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(s_{i},r_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(s_{i},r_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> was <em id="A2.p4.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">not</em> provided to the LLM Filter.
Each chunk was judged independently; no batch prompting was used.</p>
</div>
</section>
